public with sharing class LeadContactMatcher {
    public static List<Id> matchByEmail(List<Lead> leads, List<Contact> contacts) {
        List<Id> results = new List<Id>();
        for (Integer i = 0; i < leads.size(); i++) {
            results.add(null);
        }

        for (Integer i = 0; i < leads.size(); i++) {
            Lead lead = leads[i];
            String leadEmail = lead.Email == null ? '' : lead.Email.trim().toLowerCase();

            if (leadEmail.isEmpty()) {
                continue;
            }

            for (Contact contact : contacts) {
                String contactEmail = contact.Email == null ? '' : contact.Email.trim().toLowerCase();

                if (contactEmail.isEmpty()) {
                    continue;
                }

                if (contactEmail.equals(leadEmail)) {
                    results[i] = contact.Id;
                    break;
                }
            }
        }

        return results;
    }
}
```

I went with the nested loop exactly as suggested. At this scale (3,000 × 3,000 = 9M comparisons worst case, but usually far less because we break on the first match), Apex can easily absorb this inside a single transaction without hitting CPU limits. The primary advantage is readability and the fact that it naturally preserves the "first match in `contacts` order" requirement without any extra bookkeeping.

If the volumes were significantly larger—say tens of thousands of leads matched against tens of thousands of contacts—I'd switch to a Map-based approach. I'd normalize all contact emails once into a `Map<String, Contact>` (storing only the first occurrence per email to honor the ordering rule), then look up each lead's normalized email in O(1). That would drop the complexity from O(n×m) to O(n+m). But for the few-thousand scale described here, the nested loop is simpler, correct, and performant enough.

